For this project, I started by building a basic p5.js sketch with a player and some simple movement, then slowly added more features like food, obstacles, scoring, health, and a timer. I also added different game states (start, play, and game over) to control how the game flows. A big part of my process was testing things as I went and fixing problems step by step. I ran into a few issues, like my keys not working because I used the wrong operator, and problems with sprites not showing up because I was using the wrong library. Once I switched to p5.play and fixed those bugs, everything started working a lot better.

I did use ChatGPT to help me during this project, mostly when I got stuck. I used it to figure out error messages, fix syntax mistakes, and understand how to correctly use things like sprites and animations. It was helpful for getting unstuck quickly, but I still had to test everything myself and make sure it actually worked in my code. I didn’t just copy and paste everything I used it more as a guide to understand what I should change.

I didn’t directly copy code from other people, but I did follow common patterns used in p5.js, like how setup and draw work, how to structure a game loop, and how to handle collisions. I adjusted everything to fit my own game idea. Overall, this project helped me understand how to build a small interactive game and how to debug when things go wrong. If I kept working on it, I’d want to add more features like better animations, sounds, and maybe enemies to make it more interesting.
